ous can the immune  system deal with it better such studies  take a lot of time one major change in  the virus has apparently already been  determined earlier generations of  coronavirus replicated in the lungs a  new mutations using host cells somewhere  else in the body the throat more  contagious if not more deadly so what  does that mean for the search for a  vaccine our researchers struggling to  keep up with an ever-changing virus  as as Congrats double fun it completely  depends on how this vaccine is designed  whether it does end up offering a broad  immune response the virus would have a  tougher time quickly getting around a  vaccine with the wide-reaching and very  effective immune response it seems we  may not need a new vaccine for every new  year like with influenza that's at least  the hope we have for the coming years  with every new patient emerging immune  after surviving cough in nineteen  pressure piles on coronavirus to change  as it does scientists will have to keep  up earlier I spoke to dr. andreas bacala  of the Research Center for molecular  medicine of the austrian academy of  sciences and as there are now multiple  versions of the corona virus i asked  them if their characteristics were  vastly different there are hundreds of  different mutations and and this is a  totally natural and normal process  because these RNA viruses they have an  imperfect replication of their own  genome so per se just finding mutations  is nothing bad or good this is just how  nature works okay  lab research shows that the globally  dominating version of the corona virus  transmits more easily between humans now  than the original did does that mean  it's also more dangerous no it does not  mean that it's more dangerous you're  referring to one particular mutation  that has been in the media over the last  few weeks and it's known by researchers  for months already  it's called D 614 gene mutation and it  yes it is a prevalent mutation that is  found pretty much all over the globe by  now and also by now scientists have  actually obtained evidence in the lab in  vitro that this mutation might lead to  higher infectivity again in vitro but  what it actually means in a human to  human transmission scenario and for the  pandemic is still something researchers  need to work out so it transmits more  easily does that mean it's less  contagious less lethal  I think at the moment there is very  little evidence that it effects the  clinical course of infection the only  thing I guess that is more or less hard  evidence is that patients who have this  virus with this given mutation tend to  have a higher viral load and high viral  load could translate into higher  transmissibility but the actual proof  for this is still missing and out there  hmm  other ways to predict how viruses are  likely to mutate that's a difficult one  I think it's easier to look backward  retrospectively and I think these  mutation landscapes that we have  basically identified in the past have  been a wonderful tool to look back in  history where does the virus come from  and tracking its history right but  looking in a more prospective future  forward-looking perspective this is  nothing more difficult and this has to  do with the fact that mutations occur  randomly and it depends on the context  whether they might confer an advantage  to the virus or not so in that sense I  think you cannot predict exactly which  will be the next mutations coming up yet  the mutation that we have already  actually tell us a lot about the history  of the virus now as the novel  coronavirus has changed has mutated over  the last couple of months what does that  mean for the development of a vaccine of  course one may be needs to explain that  the vaccine is usually working by means  of antibodies or also by another immune  cell called t-cells and those immune  cells and antibodies they recognize  specific regions are in the virus in the  proteins of the virus and so if these  proteins would mutate away it and  potentially the target is gone and the  vaccine would not be working anymore  however what is important to to make  clear is that a vaccine never has only  one target but it has multiple targets  tens or hundreds of targets and in this  regard I think it's extremely unlikely  that one or few mutations on the virus  ID would render a vaccine effect less  and less spectral a member of the  Research Center for molecular medicine  of the  Austrian Academy of Sciences thank you  for your insights and next up is Derrick  Williams he's our science correspondent  and has been hard at work answering your  questions about kovat 19 here's our  latest installment have any researchers  tried ivermectin I talked about this a  couple of months ago but in light of  recent developments I thought it would  be good to discuss it again health  officials in some countries particularly  in in South America have been promoting  the anti-parasitic drug ivermectin as a  treatment for kovat 19and and people  have been buying up supplies of it even  though there's no solid evidence that  the medication works against the new  coronavirus ivermectin is however a very  effective treatment for infestations of  of nasty things like like lice and and  pin worms one big problem now is that  with stocks that the drugs used to treat  humans depleted people are beginning to  self dose with versions of the drug that  are usually prescribed for animals now  it should be obvious why that's a very  bad idea there are human trials going on  and at least one has been concluded but  the first results don't really look very  convincing um until we know a lot more  the WH o is not recommending ivermectin  for anything but what it was developed  for which is parasite infestations how  different is SARS Cove to two other  viruses that infect us for example do  people who catch influenza sometimes  have no symptoms yes although  asymptomatic is a is a pretty squishy  term we